= Energy — Technical =

== Classical kinetic and potential quantities ==
For a classical particle with mass m and velocity v, the kinetic energy is K = (1/2)m|v|². For a conservative interaction, a potential energy U may be defined such that changes in U account for work done by conservative forces. The total mechanical energy K + U is constant under the stated conservative-system assumptions.

== Work and energy ==
The mechanical work done by a force is the integral of the force along the displacement path:

<pre>
W = integral F · dr
ΔK = W_net
</pre>

The work–energy theorem applies under classical dynamics; it does not imply that all transfer of energy occurs as mechanical work.

== Accounting boundaries ==
In an open system, energy may cross the boundary as heat, work, radiation or matter-associated transport. The terms must be defined for the system and thermodynamic convention. A decrease in mechanical energy from friction is not a failure of total-energy conservation.

== Reference frames and advanced regimes ==
Kinetic energy depends on the reference frame. Relativistic and quantum theories use more general formulations of energy than elementary K = (1/2)mv²; the applicable regime must be specified.

